= Data structure =

== Overview ==
A '''data structure''' is an organization and representation of data designed to support particular operations. Arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues, hash tables, trees, heaps, and graphs are familiar examples.

Data structures matter because the way information is represented changes what a [[Program (computing)|program]] or [[Algorithm|algorithm]] can do efficiently. The same logical information can often be represented in several ways, each with different trade-offs.

== Definition ==
NIST describes a data structure as an organization of information, usually in memory, used for algorithmic efficiency or conceptual unity. A useful data structure normally combines:

* stored values or records;
* relationships among them;
* operations that access or modify them;
* invariants that those operations are expected to preserve.

The representation is therefore more than a collection of values. Its organization gives operations their meaning and cost.

== Data structure and abstract data type ==
A data structure should be distinguished from an '''[[Abstract data type|abstract data type]] (ADT)'''.

An ADT specifies a set of values and permitted operations according to their behavior without committing to one representation. A stack, for example, can be described by operations such as push and pop together with last-in, first-out behavior.

The stack can then be implemented with an array, linked list, persistent structure, or another data structure. The distinction is between a behavioral abstraction and a concrete or representational organization, although informal usage sometimes blurs the terms.

== Operations and invariants ==
Common operations include:

* lookup and search;
* insertion and deletion;
* traversal;
* update;
* ordering and sorting;
* merging;
* aggregation.

Many structures depend on invariants. A binary-search tree relies on an ordering relation; a heap maintains a heap-order property; a hash table relies on a mapping from keys into storage locations and a strategy for collisions.

Correct operations preserve the required invariants as the structure changes.

== Common structures ==
'''Array''' — indexed sequence, usually supporting efficient access by position.

'''Linked list''' — nodes connected by references, making some local insertions/removals convenient but arbitrary indexing slower.

'''Stack''' — last-in, first-out access.

'''Queue''' — first-in, first-out access.

'''Hash table''' — key-based storage using a hash function, generally designed for efficient expected lookup.

'''Tree''' — hierarchical structure; variants include search trees, heaps, tries, and syntax trees.

'''Graph''' — entities connected by arbitrary relationships; used for networks, routes, dependencies, social relationships, knowledge graphs, and many other domains.

== Relationship to algorithms ==
Algorithms and data structures are often designed together. A representation determines which operations are cheap, what information is immediately available, and which invariants can be exploited.

For example, graph traversal has different memory and access behavior depending on whether a graph is represented by adjacency lists, matrices, edge lists, compressed structures, or distributed partitions.

== Complexity and cost models ==
The cost of operations may be analyzed as worst-case, average-case, expected, or amortized complexity.

But asymptotic operation counts are not the only relevant costs. Real systems may be dominated by cache locality, disk access, memory bandwidth, synchronization, network communication, persistence, or energy. Performance claims should identify the relevant cost model and assumptions.

== Advanced forms ==
Important families include:

* persistent and immutable structures;
* concurrent data structures;
* external-memory structures;
* cache-aware and cache-oblivious structures;
* succinct and compressed structures;
* probabilistic structures;
* distributed structures.

These extend the same central idea: choose an organization that supports the required operations under a particular computational environment.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Data structure" is established terminology, but theoretical computer science, programming languages, databases, and systems engineering emphasize different aspects. Avoid declaring a structure universally "fast" without naming the operation, input assumptions, representation, and resource model.

